{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2345 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c4da700b5b20c89d1024d48542ed96fcbf67f26bda6a165b6f0e6174f627b652" :edb/db-before-t 2344 :edb/db-after-t 2345 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535657 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:33:50.273Z" 13194139535657 true] [17592186221115 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." 13194139535657 true] [17592186221115 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = y^{2}(t) - x(t)$\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." 13194139535657 false] [17592186221115 1145 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x - 2y = 0; y^{2} - x = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx - 2y = 0⇒y = \\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n(\\frac{x}{2})^{2} - x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} - x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x - 4) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0$, we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0$.\n- For $x=4$, we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535657 false] [17592186221115 1145 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068805/assets/9cff385cda8e9a4cac784cb4212fee9ec1e65f48a1ebd05f9af75f71aead66aa.png)" 13194139535657 true] [17592186221116 1100 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n- Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" 13194139535657 true] [17592186221116 1100 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\n1. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n2. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n3. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n4. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$." 13194139535657 false] [17592186221116 1145 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O$, approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty$.In plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$, there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$, such that every orbit, which begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$, remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0$.In plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it's not stable.In plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\n> Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty$." 13194139535657 false] [17592186221116 1145 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" 13194139535657 true] [17592186221352 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359479 13194139535657 true] [17592186221352 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359486 13194139535657 true] [17592186221352 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359493 13194139535657 true] [17592186221352 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359500 13194139535657 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359435 13194139535657 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359448 13194139535657 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359462 13194139535657 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359472 13194139535657 true] [17592186221896 1017 #edb/ref 17592186220316 13194139535657 false] [17592186221896 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359442 13194139535657 true] [17592186221896 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535657 true] [17592186221896 1100 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-y(t)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139535657 true] [17592186221896 1100 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) + y(t) + 1; y^{′}(t) = 2x(t) - y(t) - 1$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139535657 false] [17592186221896 1145 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ 2x-y-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3y-3=0\\,⇒\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+1=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535657 true] [17592186221899 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535657 true] [17592186221899 1145 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Unstable.\"" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359436 13194139535657 true] [17592186359435 1089 "q-332968" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535657 true] [17592186359435 1100 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359435 1113 #uuid "6b6b058e-9ff1-5f78-9443-7901f71ac9f8" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359435 1145 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359437 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359438 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359439 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359440 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359441 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1041 "selection" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359438 13194139535657 true] [17592186359436 1110 #uuid "b35eed4d-7668-54c5-ad4e-fb519364521c" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359437 1034 "Solutions converge to the equilibrium only if the system is linear." 13194139535657 true] [17592186359437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535657 true] [17592186359437 1111 #uuid "1a3802a9-0f54-53e4-8f51-5dd89c864e42" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359438 1034 "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and con