O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{and converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359450 13194139535657 true] [17592186359449 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359451 13194139535657 true] [17592186359449 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535657 true] [17592186359449 1041 "field-1" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359449 1104 #edb/ref 17592186359450 13194139535657 true] [17592186359449 1110 #uuid "3517b8ea-67d1-585b-b3ba-b6be985baf15" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359450 1034 "close to" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535657 true] [17592186359450 1111 #uuid "c86631ba-5345-569f-85c1-a80b34b80210" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359451 1034 "away from" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359451 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535657 true] [17592186359451 1111 #uuid "a5afa473-578e-5078-b850-3d0a17657cfa" 13194139535657 true] [17592186359452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186359453 13194139